## C67 — the C-II force hull has a fixed positive area fraction

After C65, the response boundary is the full actual diameter-endpoint set. C34 gives

`2|B| >= ceil(D)+2`,

hence the full diameter graph has more than `D/4` edges.

Any two distinct diameter edges have projective direction distance at least

`delta_D = 2 asin(1/(2D))`.

For shared-endpoint edges this is the unit-separation chord bound. For disjoint diameter edges, the diameter-thrackle crossing geometry gives the stronger `phi>=asin(1/D)`.

The stale PR #42 used a false projective-circle statement at the `pi/2` threshold. The corrected packing lemma is:

`h` pairwise `delta`-separated points on a circle of circumference `pi` have two points at distance at least

`min(pi/3,(h-1)delta)`.

The `pi/3` threshold is sufficient because a set of circular diameter below `pi/3` lies in one ordinary arc of that same length.

Therefore two actual diameter chords have projective angle at least

`Phi(D)=min(pi/3,(D/4-1)2asin(1/(2D)))`.

Whether those two diameter chords share an endpoint or cross as disjoint diameter edges, the convex hull of their endpoints has area exactly

`(D^2/2) sin(phi)`.

Hence

`area(F) >= (D^2/2) sin Phi(D)`

and

`liminf area(F)/D^2 >= (1/2)sin(1/4) = 0.1237019796...`.

So the remaining C-II carrier is quantitatively two-dimensional even if C66 forces its normalized diameter stress to concentrate on one star.

## Next target

Combine C66 stress-star concentration with the now-quantitative two-dimensional carrier. A viable closing estimate must charge the `Omega(D)` low-weight diameter directions or their required contact-network branching, rather than treating the source as an almost one-dimensional stress chain.
